<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maki Izumi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maki Yasuomi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maki Izumi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> priest of the Suitengu shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurume">Kurume</a> and a leading imperial loyalist of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. He promoted the restoration of direct imperial rule and the expulsion of foreigners, and allied himself with the radicals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> domain. After the Choshu attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> failed in the Kinmon Incident of 1864, he killed himself on Mount Tenno.</p></div>
